## Overview

This file is the single source of truth for the project's visual identity. Every UI
surface — landing pages, dashboards, marketing site, mobile app — derives its tokens
from the YAML front-matter above and respects the rationale below. Coding agents read
this file before generating any frontend code, and the design gate
(`tools/design_gate.py`) reads it before letting a visual surface be declared done.

## Aesthetic Family

`aesthetic_family` is the first decision, and every other decision on this page is
downstream of it. It is declared, not implied. This template declares **F3 — Warm
Editorial**: a serif body on cream with a single terracotta accent, for products that
should feel made by people rather than assembled by a framework.

If your product is a developer tool, a dashboard, or a statement piece, F3 is the
wrong family and the tokens below are the wrong tokens. Run the three-question picker
in CDIO-06 sec. 2 and replace them. Changing the accent hex while leaving `F3` in
place is how a design system quietly becomes a default again.

## Colors

Warm neutrals with one evocative accent. Temperature is the character here: cream and
clay carry it, and a cool grey would erase it.

- **Primary (#191817):** clay-black for headlines and core text. Permanence, readability.
- **Secondary (#6b665f):** warm slate for utility — borders, captions, metadata. Never competes with primary.
- **Accent (#c96442):** terracotta, reserved for interaction — primary CTAs, links, focus rings. Exactly one accent hue survives; a second is indecision (CDIO-06 sec. 3, rule 2).
- **Neutral (#f4f3ee):** the page ground. Cream, not white — the single largest carrier of warmth on the page.
- **Error (#a33a28):** state semantics only. Desaturated so it belongs to this palette instead of shouting over it. Do not repurpose for branding.

Contrast: every color used as text must clear WCAG AA (≥ 4.5:1 body, ≥ 3:1 large text).
This is a floor, not a target, and it is not arbitrable against character (CDIO-06 sec. 3, rule 5).

## Typography

Three families, each with a job: **Instrument Serif** for display, **Lora** for body,
**Work Sans** for UI chrome, plus **JetBrains Mono** where code appears.

- **Headlines (h1, h2):** Instrument Serif at regular weight. Editorial authority without institutional stiffness; the low weight at large sizes is the restraint that keeps it from shouting.
- **Body (body-md):** Lora at 17px. A serif body is the F3 commitment — it is what makes long-form reading feel like reading rather than scanning.
- **Labels (label-sm):** Work Sans medium at 13px with positive tracking, for metadata, captions, and form helpers. The one place a grotesque earns its place: chrome should recede.
- **Mono:** JetBrains Mono at 14px for code and tabular data.

Line-height contracts as size grows (1.05 at h1, 1.65 at body) — a deliberate hierarchy signal.

No default-tier font stack (Inter, Roboto, Arial, system-ui) appears here, because F3
does not sanction one. Three of the nine families do sanction Inter, deliberately and
for stated reasons; F3 is not among them. Inheriting a framework's font is not a
typographic decision, and `check_font_stack` will fail it.

## Layout

Strict 8px grid; every spacing token is a multiple of 4px. Page max-width `min(1280px, 92vw)`.
Section rhythm: `xl` (32px) between top-level sections, `lg` (24px) within a section.
Nesting depth is capped at two semantic levels — stacked padding with no semantic
purpose is the container-soup fingerprint (CDIO-06 sec. 4).

## Elevation & Depth

Depth comes from tonal layers and borders, not heavy shadows. The default surface sits
on the cream ground; elevated surfaces use the off-white surface token with a 1px
secondary border at 12% opacity.

## Shapes

Architectural softness — `md` (8px) for buttons, inputs, cards. `lg` (12px) for hero
panels. `full` reserved for avatars and pill badges only. Sharp corners are forbidden
on interactive surfaces.

## Motion & Experience

The numbers live in the `experience:` block above; this section says why they are
those numbers. Restating a value here would create a second source that drifts, so
nothing below repeats one.

Motion clarifies flow; it does not decorate. One well-orchestrated entrance with
staggered reveals beats scattered micro-interactions. Hover, focus, and active states
must all feel designed — an interactive element with no considered focus state is an
accessibility failure, not a styling omission. No animated status indicators.

The declared ceiling is `restrained` because this is a reading surface: the user came
for the content, and every animation competes with it. That ceiling is enforced in
both directions. A control that does not acknowledge a click inside the declared
latency fails; so does a component whose motion exceeds the declared budget, and so
does a celebration on an event `celebration_policy` excludes. An axis that could only
ever ask for more animation would be a preference wearing a gate's clothes (CDIO-07 §4).

`reduced_motion: equivalent` means the information and the state change still arrive
by a non-motion channel — a cross-fade replacing a slide is conformance; a shortened
animation is not. This is an accessibility floor and it is not arbitrable against
character (CDIO-06 §3 rule 5, restated in CDIO-07 §5).

Two collisions worth naming before they are discovered as a BLOCK: a celebration on an
operation that is queued rather than settled asserts a state the system has not
reached, which is a trust defect and not a delight; and numeric progress synthesised
from nothing is worse than declaring it indeterminate.

If this product is a console, an admin table, or a compliance surface, the correct
contract is `expressiveness: none` — a complete, passing declaration, not an
unfinished one.

## Components

Component tokens reference primitives via `{path.to.token}` — never hardcode a color at
the component level. State variants suffix the component name (`-hover`, `-active`,
`-disabled`).

## Component Provenance

Nine decisions about code this project did not write. They are not commentary: together
they **are** the context object the CDICF selector consumes
(`node modules/cdicf/selector.js --context <this, as JSON>`), so a decision recorded here
changes what the engine will recommend, filter, or refuse. A decision left unrecorded is
not a neutral default — it is a filter that never runs.

1. **Adoption posture — `fork_canonical`.** Components are copied in and owned, not
   wrapped. A fork we maintain beats an adapter over an upstream whose API we do not
   control. Reuse-first still applies: ask the selector before building
   (`DESIGN_GOVERNANCE.md` §8.1).
2. **Licence floor — `PERMISSIVE` only.** `WEAK_COPYLEFT` requires a decision per
   component; `SOURCE_AVAILABLE_RESTRICTED` and `PROPRIETARY` are reachable **only** as
   code-free upstream pointers, never vendored.
3. **Attribution — `can_attribute: true`.** This project ships a NOTICE surface and can
   carry a required notice. A project that cannot must say so, because it makes every
   notice-requiring component inadmissible and the engine should refuse them up front
   rather than at legal review.
4. **Redistribution intent — this project does not ship its own component library.** If it
   did, every restriction would propagate to its consumers, and the admissible set would
   narrow accordingly. State this before adopting, not after publishing.
5. **Bundle budget — 40 kB per surface.** Absolute, not a share of the total: a percentage
   budget is satisfied by the bundle growing. When nothing fits, the answer is nothing —
   the selector abstains with `REMEDY_BUDGET` rather than picking the least-bad overspend.
6. **Accessibility floor — WCAG AA.** A hard filter, not a scored preference. `unassessed`
   is **not** a pass: an unmeasured level treated as neutral is how an unknown quietly
   becomes a yes.
7. **Rendering model — SSR yes, RSC no.** This is a hard compatibility filter. A
   client-only component in an SSR project does not rank low; it is removed, because it
   breaks at render rather than merely disappointing.
8. **Motion budget — declared once, in `experience.motion_budget`.** Ceiling, not a
   target. A component whose own motion budget exceeds it is filtered, and
   `reduced_motion_compliant` is required at every level above `none`. The value is not
   repeated here: this decision and the experience contract are the same decision, and
   two copies of one decision is one decision plus a drift surface. Emit the context the
   selector reads with `python tools/design_gate.py --emit-context ./DESIGN.md` rather
   than hand-writing it — a context object typed by hand is a filter that silently
   disagrees with the document it claims to represent.
9. **Onboarding posture — guidance is a last resort.** Open usability findings are recorded
   in `unresolved_ux_findings`, and while any stand, a tour request returns
   `REMEDY_FIX_UX_FIRST` instead of a component. A walkthrough over a broken interface
   conceals the problem and adds a dependency.

Every third-party component additionally carries a Component Manifest with a pinned commit
and licence fingerprint (`DESIGN_GOVERNANCE.md` §8.2). Provenance travels with the artifact;
a side document drifts.

## Do's and Don'ts

- Do declare `aesthetic_family` before writing a single token.
- Do use the accent for the single most important action per screen.
- Don't mix rounded and sharp corners in one view.
- Do maintain WCAG AA contrast for all text — the floor is not tradeable.
- Don't use more than two font families in a single viewport.
- Don't inherit a font from a framework and call it a choice.
- Do run the selector before building a component that might already exist.
- Don't adopt a component whose licence, holder, or commit you cannot name.
- Don't rename external code to obscure where it came from.
